WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.980
The action film "The Misfits” follows a
group of vigilantes who convince prison escape

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:11.030
artist Richard Pace, played by Pierce Brosnan,
to help them pull off a gold heist in the

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:13.640
fictional country of Jazeristan.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:19.910
The gold is originally meant to finance terrorist
activities, and a man named Schultz is storing

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:25.210
the fortune for a terrorist organization in
one of his many high-security prisons.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:30.010
With a team of misfit criminals, including
con artist Ringo, skilled fighter Violet,

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:35.470
and the mysterious Prince, Pace and company
hatch a plan to steal their bounty from a

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.899
Jazeristan penitentiary.

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:43.260
Their basic intention is to give the entire
prison population severe food poisoning, dress

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.570
up as medical personnel, then sneak out with
the gold.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:47.739
A simple plan?

00:00:47.739 --> 00:00:49.200
Not exactly.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.031
Let's dive into the multiple twists at the
end of "The Misfits."

00:00:53.031 --> 00:00:55.009
"Steal their gold, stop the terrorists.

00:00:55.009 --> 00:00:57.329
Let's go to prison, shall we?"

00:00:57.329 --> 00:01:02.280
The personal antagonism between Schultz and
Richard Pace is obvious from the beginning

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:07.159
of the movie due to the heavy implication
that Pace previously slept with Schultz's

00:01:07.159 --> 00:01:08.159
wife.

00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:13.859
Schultz therefore has a vendetta against Pace,
and Pace reciprocates the rivalry by humiliating

00:01:13.859 --> 00:01:15.759
Schultz whenever possible.

00:01:15.759 --> 00:01:20.579
This motivation explains the reasoning behind
the numerous outlandish diversions and red

00:01:20.579 --> 00:01:22.430
herrings during the robbery.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:28.140
First, by giving the entire prison population
food poisoning, the team causes hundreds of

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.599
inmates to suffer intense vomiting and intestinal
distress.

00:01:32.599 --> 00:01:37.250
This throws the institution into chaos, and
allows the robbers to take advantage of the

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.649
distracted staff during the pandemonium.

00:01:39.649 --> 00:01:44.719
However, for Pace, the health of the convicts
is simply a means to an end.

00:01:44.719 --> 00:01:50.469
When Wick expresses concern about their well-being,
Pace heartlessly brushes off the matter.

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:56.369
Pace's main objective was to embarrass Schultz
in his own facility, regardless of the collateral

00:01:56.369 --> 00:02:00.709
damage to hundreds of people who have nothing
to do with his personal vendetta.

00:02:00.709 --> 00:02:06.080
Secondly, it's eventually revealed that the
gold bars weren't transported as bricks, but

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.829
instead melted down into the shape of a statue.

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:15.650
While Schultz's team chases after a van they
presume is carrying the riches, the real gold

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:20.129
is wheeled out of the facility under the pretense
of repairing the sculpture.

00:02:20.129 --> 00:02:24.950
This means Schultz is further disgraced when
he catches up to the van, only to find it

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:25.950
empty.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:31.670
Once Schultz does catch the van, Pace's crew
is outmanned and outgunned — that is, until

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:37.390
a fleet of soldiers with a military aircraft
inexplicably emerge from the sand dunes, despite

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.080
there being no cover for miles around.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.290
These men are supposedly under the command
of the Prince.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:49.280
The regiment surrounds Schultz, claiming to
be from the fictional sovereign nation of

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.280
Salwhan.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.379
Whether this is another trick, or a reveal
that the Prince really is royalty remains

00:02:55.379 --> 00:03:00.220
somewhat ambiguous, but the tactic does allow
them to leave unharmed.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:05.230
What isn't ambiguous, however, is that the
vigilante thieves condemn Schultz to death

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:10.969
by divulging his whereabouts to Abu Hirawa,
the head of the terrorist organization they

00:03:10.969 --> 00:03:12.379
just robbed.

00:03:12.379 --> 00:03:17.549
As to why Hirawa would punish his lackey in
charge of protecting the gold instead of going

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:22.550
after those directly responsible for swindling
his wealth standing right in front of him,

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:25.090
that remains an unanswered question.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:29.849
Even if Hirawa believed that the Prince was
royalty from a sovereign nation, he has no

00:03:29.849 --> 00:03:33.489
reason to care about the status of the thieves
who stole his treasure.

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:38.280
For that matter, if the group really was trying
to make the world a better place and stop

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.760
terrorism, why didn't they kill Hirawa right
then?

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.549
They had a literal squadron of soldiers surrounding
the area; they could have brought down the

00:03:46.549 --> 00:03:52.110
head of an international terrorist organization
and destabilized his entire establishment.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:57.189
They already displayed that they're not above
killing people, as Violet has killed a number

00:03:57.189 --> 00:04:01.810
of guards during the course of the film, so
why not one more death for the cause?

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:03.719
"I don't date men.

00:04:03.719 --> 00:04:05.170
I kill them."

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:10.389
For the final bait-and-switch of "The Misfits,"
Schultz's goons are holding Hope hostage,

00:04:10.389 --> 00:04:15.200
and just as she's about to be executed, it's
revealed that Violet was the one being held

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:20.780
captive, which spells bad news for those guys
as she slays her abductors with ease.

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:25.810
How these kidnappers mistook a blonde white
woman for a brunette Korean American is another

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:27.560
unsolved mystery.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.240
Were they not given a general description?

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.890
Or a photo from her Instagram?

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:36.230
Similarly, in the penultimate scene of the
movie, it appears that Richard Pace has gone

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:41.750
back on his word and stolen the gold for himself,
but he returns to the team remorseful, the

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:43.620
gilded statue in tow.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.270
When watching "The Misfits," the audience
is in the same shoes as Schultz.

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:52.830
Due to the many false trails the script sets
up, it's easy to become just as confused about

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.590
what's going on as the people being robbed.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:05:01.590
Though one or two plot twists can be exciting,
too many curveballs tend to result in whiplash.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:06.889
But that's the narrative's entire intention:
to keep us on our toes, throwing us from point

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:09.350
to point, and keep us constantly guessing.

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:14.199
If that's the goal of "The Misfits,” then
mission accomplished.

